 {
  "event_id": "74fd9b1b-efe3-4f33-a614-6603490b218d",
  "type": "conversation.item.added",
  "item": {
   "id": "464ba669-5653-45c2-a266-df34aa57435e",
   "object": "realtime.item",
   "type": "function_call_output",
   "call_id": "chatcmpl-tool-503606ac61c64a86bc618e29c26406fb",
   "output": "[1] Tf-idf weighting\n    https://nlp.stanford.edu/IR-book/html/htmledition/tf-idf-weighting-1.html\n    ting We now combine the definitions of term frequency and inverse document frequency, to produce a composite weight for each term in each document. The _tf-idf_ weighting scheme assigns to term $t$ a weight in document $d$ given by |\\begin{displaymath} \\mbox{tf-idf}_{t,d} = \\mbox{tf}_{t,d} \\times \\mbox{idf}_t. \\end{displaymath} |(22) |\n[2] IDF term weighting and IR research lessons Karen Sparck Jones Computer Laboratory, University of Cambridge This paper appeared in Journal\n    https://www.cl.cam.ac.uk/archive/ksj21/ksjdigipapers/jdoc04.pdf\n    **IDF term weighting and IR research lessons** Karen Sparck Jones Computer Laboratory, University of Cambridge This paper appeared in _Journal of Documentation_ , 60, 2004, 521-523. **Abstract** practice. It is an honour to have the small proposal for term weighting that I published more than thirty years ago (Sparck Jones 1972) the subject of Stephen Robertson\u2019s paper (Robertson 2004).\n[3] The Vector Space Model in Information Retrieval - Term Weighting Problem\n    https://wiki.eecs.yorku.ca/course_archive/2014-15/W/6339/_media/polettini_information_retrieval.pdf\n    This formula was proposed by Croft [4] and parameterized by a value equal to K. Croft suggested that K must be set to something low (0.3) for large documents and to higher values (0.5) for shorter have low IDF. For example we obtain an output value eqaul to 0 if the given term appears in every document. The weight increases as the number of documents in wich the term appears decreases.\n[4] Understanding Inverse Document Frequency: On theoretical arguments for IDF\n    https://dmice.ohsu.edu/bedricks/courses/cs635_spring_2017/pdf/robertson_2004.pdf\n    **Abstract** The term weighting function known as IDF was proposed in 1972, and has since been extremely widely used, usually as part of a TF\\*IDF function. its application in retrieval\u201d (Sparck Jones, 1972). The measure of term specificity first proposed in that paper later became known as _inverse document frequency_ , or IDF; it is based on counting\n[5] Inverse document frequency\n    https://nlp.stanford.edu/IR-book/html/htmledition/inverse-document-frequency-1.html\n    An immediate idea is to scale down the term weights of terms with high _collection frequency,_ defined to be the total number of occurrences of a term in the collection. The idea would be to reduce the $\\mbox{tf}$ weight of a term by a factor that grows with its collection frequency.",
   "status": null
  },
  "previous_item_id": "3d615022-ac75-4dc1-af50-88dbdf421160",
  "_t": 145648.718
